  <article class="container">
    <span class="tag">AI Agents</span>
    <h1>Xiaomi MiMo Desktop: The AI Agent That Does Your Work — Beta Now Open</h1>
    <p class="meta">Published September 9, 2026 · ToolStep Editorial Team · Based on Xiaomi MiMo's official announcement and product pages</p>

    <p>On <strong>September 8, 2026</strong>, Xiaomi opened invitation-only beta access for <strong>MiMo Desktop</strong> — a desktop AI agent that does not just talk about work, but is built to complete it. You give it input in multiple formats and a goal; it understands the objective, decomposes it into tasks, calls the tools it needs, executes a real workflow, and hands back an editable result. That last part — <em>editable deliverables, not chat replies</em> — is what separates MiMo Desktop from the chatbots most people use daily.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Status check:</strong> MiMo Desktop is in <strong>invitation-only beta</strong>. The companion models <strong>MiMo-X-Pro-Preview</strong> and <strong>MiMo-X-Flash-Preview</strong> are previews. Nothing in this announcement is a general-availability release — treat all capabilities as beta behavior until Xiaomi says otherwise.
    </div>

    <h2>What Is MiMo Desktop?</h2>
    <p>MiMo Desktop is Xiaomi's desktop AI workflow agent. Where a chatbot answers questions in a conversation window, MiMo Desktop is positioned as a work surface: it accepts input across multiple formats, interprets what you are trying to accomplish, breaks the goal into structured tasks, routes those tasks through its agent architecture, invokes the tools required at each step, and produces finished, editable output. The officially listed workflow directions span <strong>documents, spreadsheets, slides, web pages, images, audio, video, 3D, and apps/software engineering projects</strong> — a scope that covers most of what knowledge workers produce.</p>

    <h2>Why This Is Different from a Chatbot</h2>
    <p>The practical difference is the shape of the output. A chatbot gives you text you must copy, reformat, and assemble into the actual deliverable. MiMo Desktop's loop ends with the deliverable itself: a formatted document, a working spreadsheet, a slide deck, a web page, or a software project you can open and edit. That shift — from answers to artifacts — is the same direction the whole agent market is moving, and Xiaomi's implementation concentrates it on the desktop, where the files and tools already live.</p>

    <AdUnit slot="2222222222" format="rectangle" layout="responsive" />

    <h2>How the Agent Actually Executes Work</h2>
    <p>Per Xiaomi's announcement, MiMo Desktop's execution stack is organized around three routing layers: <strong>Harness / Agent / Skill</strong>. A submitted goal is decomposed into tasks automatically; each task is routed to the appropriate agent and skill; and the agents coordinate to carry the work through. Along the way the system performs <strong>tool calling</strong> — invoking the specific capabilities a step requires — and manages <strong>long-task cost optimization</strong>, which matters because multi-step agent runs are where token costs usually balloon.</p>

    <h2>Browser Control and Multi-Agent Collaboration</h2>
    <p>Two capabilities stand out in the announcement. First, <strong>browser control</strong>: the agent can operate a browser, which is what makes web-research, web-page production, and online workflows automatable rather than merely describable. Second, <strong>multi-agent collaboration with multi-session support</strong>: separate agents (and sessions) can work in parallel and hand results to each other, which is how a single high-level goal like "build and publish a landing page for this product" becomes research plus copywriting plus page construction without you orchestrating each hop.</p>

    <h2>Supported Workflow Types</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Workflow direction</th><th>What the agent delivers</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Documents</td><td>Drafts, rewrites, and structured documents as editable files</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Spreadsheets</td><td>Data organization, calculation, and analysis workbooks</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Slides</td><td>Presentation decks with structure and content</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Web pages</td><td>Researched and built pages, using browser control for source material</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Images / audio / video</td><td>Generated and processed media assets inside the workflow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3D</td><td>3D content as part of supported workflows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Apps / software engineering</td><td>Code projects and engineering tasks as working software</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The MiMo-X Model Previews</h2>
    <p>Alongside the desktop agent, Xiaomi introduced two companion models: <strong>MiMo-X-Pro-Preview</strong>, the higher-capability preview for complex work, and <strong>MiMo-X-Flash-Preview</strong>, the faster, lighter preview. Both are explicitly previews — their benchmarks and behavior should be read as directional, and Xiaomi has not announced general availability dates for either. For beta users, the practical takeaway is that agent quality will likely improve quickly as these models iterate.</p>

    <AdsterraBanner pageType="reviews" />

    <h2>Who Should Pay Attention</h2>
    <p><strong>Operations, marketing, and finance professionals</strong> who live in documents, spreadsheets, and slides are the primary audience — those are exactly the deliverable types in the announcement. <strong>Developers</strong> should watch the apps/software engineering direction and the browser-control capability, which overlap with what AI coding agents are doing. <strong>AI tool evaluators</strong> should pay attention because Xiaomi entering desktop agents with an in-house model family changes the competitive picture: agent capability is being bundled closer to the OS and the consumer hardware base.</p>

    <h2>Current Limitations and Beta Caveats</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Invitation-only:</strong> access is not open; you need an invite through the official pages.</li>
      <li><strong>Beta maturity:</strong> workflows that demo well in announcements can still fail on real files — verify outputs before relying on them.</li>
      <li><strong>Preview models:</strong> MiMo-X-Pro-Preview and MiMo-X-Flash-Preview are previews; capability and pricing may change.</li>
      <li><strong>Unknowns:</strong> the announcement does not detail data-handling terms, usage limits, or eventual pricing — all material questions for production use.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Apply for the Beta</h2>
    <p>Apply through Xiaomi's official MiMo Desktop invite pages — overseas users: <a href="https://mimo-ai.xiaomimimo.com/desktop/invite/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">mimo-ai.xiaomimimo.com/desktop/invite/</a>; domestic users: <a href="https://mimo.xiaomimimo.com/desktop/invite/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">mimo.xiaomimimo.com/desktop/invite/</a>. Access is invitation-based, so approval is not guaranteed. Apply only through these official pages — beware of third-party sites claiming to offer MiMo Desktop access.</p>

    <h2>ToolStep Editorial Takeaway</h2>
    <p>MiMo Desktop is Xiaomi making a clear claim: the desktop is where AI agents will actually do economic work, and the winner will be whoever closes the loop from goal to editable deliverable most reliably. The announced capability set — multi-agent routing, browser control, and a deliverables-first design — is the right target list. But this is a beta with preview models and unknown terms, so the right move for most readers is to apply, test it against one real recurring workflow, and judge it on delivered files rather than demo videos.</p>

